{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Elohim saw the light, that it was good</b>. Our Rabbi Shlomo [Rashi] wrote: here too we need the words of Aggadah — He saw that it was not fit for the wicked to use, and set it apart for the righteous in the time to come. And according to its plain sense, he explained it thus: He saw it, that it was good, and it is not proper for it and for darkness to be used in mixture, so He established the boundary of this one by day and the boundary of that one by night. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (on Genesis 1:4): וַיַּרְא — like \"and I saw\" (Ecclesiastes 2:13), and it is in the thought of the heart. And the sense of וַיַּבְדֵּל — by the calling of names. And the words of neither of them are correct: for if so, it would appear like the notion of deliberation and a new counsel, as though He said: for after Elohim said, \"Let there be light,\" and there was light, He saw it, that it was good, and therefore He separated between it and the darkness — like the case of a person who does not know the nature of a thing until it comes into being. But the order in the Work of Creation is that the bringing forth of things into actuality is called \"saying\": \"And Elohim said, Let there be light\" (here); \"And … said, Let there be a firmament\" (Genesis 1:6); \"And … said, Let the earth bring forth\" (Genesis 1:11). And their continued existence is called \"seeing,\" like \"and I saw\" in Ecclesiastes (2:13); and likewise \"And the woman saw that the tree was good for food\" (Genesis 3:6). And it is like the notion they said (Ketubot 109a): \"I see the words of Admon,\" and similar to it (II Samuel 15:27): \"And the king said to Zadok the priest, Do you see? Return to the city in peace.\" And the meaning is to indicate that their standing is by His will, and if the will were to depart from them for a moment — they would become nothing. And just as He said in all the works of each and every day: \"And Elohim saw that it was good,\" and on the sixth, when all was completed: \"And Elohim saw everything that He had made, and behold, it was very good\" (Genesis 1:31), so He said on the first day when the light came to be: And Elohim saw that it was good — that He desired its existence forever. And here He added אֶת הָאוֹר. For if He had said simply: And Elohim saw that it was good, it would have referred back to the creation of the heavens and the earth; and He had not yet decreed upon them continued existence, for they did not stand as such — rather, from the matter created at first, the firmament was made on the second day, and on the third the waters and the dust were separated and the dry land was formed, which He called \"earth,\" and then He decreed upon them continued existence and said of them: \"And Elohim saw that it was good.\"" "<b>And Elohim separated between the light and between the darkness</b>. This is not the darkness mentioned in the first verse, which is fire, but rather it is the cessation of the light; for Elohim gave a measure to the light, and that it would be absent thereafter until its return. And some of the commentators said that this light was created before Him, may He be blessed — that is, in the west — and was immediately set [to sink] for the measure of night, and afterward shone for the measure of day. And this is the sense of \"and there was evening and there was morning\" (Genesis 1:5) — that first there was night and then day, both after the coming-into-being of the light. And this is not correct at all, for it would add onto the six days of Creation a short day. But it is possible to say that the light was created before Him, may He be blessed, and did not extend over the elements mentioned. And He separated between it and the darkness, in that He gave a measure to both. And it stood before Him for the measure of night, and afterward He caused it to shine upon the elements. And so evening preceded morning. And it is further possible to say that from the time the heavens and the earth emerged from nothingness into existence, as mentioned in the first verse, time came into being. For even though our times are in moments and hours, which are in light and darkness, once there is existence — time can be grasped in it. And if so, the heavens and earth were created, and they stood for the measure of night without light, and He said \"Let there be light,\" and there was light, and He decreed upon it that it would stand for the first measure, and thereafter depart from the elements; \"and there was evening and there was morning.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The quotation from Rashi includes two views: first the Aggadah, then 'according to its plain sense' (ולפי פשוטו); both are attributed by Ramban to Rashi, matching the structure of Rashi's commentary on this verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:26:54.503614755Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}